          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong> Relevance logics
            , also known as
            = succeed "," do
              %strong relevant logics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logics"} classical logics
            designed to avoid what some consider to be paradoxical about classical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tautologies"} tautologies
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            implies
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            gives us reason to accept the truth of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %h2#general_description General description
          %p
            There are a number of logics that have been considered in the context of “relevance”. Many of them can, at least roughly, be regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logics"} substructural logics
            like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+logic"} affine logic
            which permits weakening but not contraction.
          %p
            Generally speaking, most relevance logics contain the following
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connectives"} logical connectives
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} logical conjunction
              and a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} logical disjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
              %em additive
              connectives, like those written
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi &amp;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
              in linear logic.
            %li
              An
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              %em multiplicative
              or
              %em intensional
              connective, like the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+implication"} linear implication
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
              of linear logic.
            %li
              A
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              but sometimes
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+law"} double negation law
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim\sim A\to A
              holds. It follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
              satisfy all the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+laws"} de Morgan laws
              with respect to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
          %p Other connectives that sometimes appear in relevance logics include:
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biconditional"} biconditional
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↔
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\leftrightarrow B
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\to B) \wedge (B\to A)
            %li
              A multiplicative
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to A
            %li
              An additive truth and an additive falsity, like linear logic’s
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0}
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
              like linear logic’s
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              %em fusion
              or
              %em cotenability
              and denoted by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim (A\to\sim B)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\circ B \to C
              if and only if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to (B\to C)
            %li
              More rarely, a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+disjunction"} multiplicative disjunction
              like linear logic’s
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⅋
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \invamp
              %em fission
              (but for which there does not seem to be a standard notation). This is also classically definable from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim A \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim\sim A\to A
              is then equivalent to multiplicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⅋
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\invamp \sim A
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            %li
              Also rarely (and apparently controversially), an additive negation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\vee \neg A
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
              akin to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \to A
              also sometimes behaves like necessity (note the mismatch of the additive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              with the multiplicative
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
          %p
            This description may give the impression that relevance logics could be obtained from linear logic by adding contraction and perhaps removing some undesired connectives. This is close to true, but many relevance logics have additional features that this would not capture. One notable one is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
            still
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributes
            over the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \wedge (B\vee C) \leftrightarrow (A\wedge B) \vee (A\wedge C)
          %p
            although this is not true in linear logic, and simply adding contraction does not make it true. (It is true linearly — and relevance-ly — that the multiplicative conjunction distributes over additive disjunction, but adding contraction does not collapse the two conjunctions.) It can of course be added as an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            that makes it true automatically, by using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunches"} bunches
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#Mints"} Mints
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunched+logic"} bunched logic
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+logic"} display logic
          %p
            Some relevance logics also do without contraction. This has been found particularly helpful in making them
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paraconsistent+logic"} paraconsistent/nontrivial
            even in the presence of things like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehension+rule"} comprehension rule
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                naive set theory
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/naive+set+theory"} ?
          %h2#some_relevance_logics Some relevance logics
          %p
            Historically, relevance logics have usually been formulated first as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+systems"} Hilbert systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            systems been found.
          %h3#the_logic_r The logic R
          %p
            The logic R+ (“
            %strong> positive R
            ”) contains
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo ,
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge,\vee,\to
            only, with contraction and the distributive law. Fusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            is sometimes added, since it can be characterized as an adjoint to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunches"} bunches
            on the left whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+connectives"} structural connectives
            represent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Mints"} Mints
          %p
            The logic R adds to R+ a multiplicative classical negation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
            (which can be shown to be a conservative extension) then it can be represented in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+logic"} display logic
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunched+implication"} bunched implication
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+system"} Hilbert system
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-relevance/logicr.html"} SEP
          %h2#categorical_semantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            Just as linear logic (with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exchange+rule"} exchange rule
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
            (both perhaps with extra structure, such as being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            or
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+categories"} semicartesian monoidal categories
            while relevance logics (with contraction) have semantics in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+monoidal+categories"} relevance monoidal categories
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunched+logic"} bunched logic
            %li
              Some relevance logics are also
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paraconsistent+logic"} paraconsistent
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Mints
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-relevance/"} Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
            %li
              %p
                G. E. Mints. Cut-elimination theorem for relevant logics, Zap. Nauchn. Sem. LOMI, 1972, Volume 32, Pages 90–97. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/archive.phtml?wshow=paper&jrnid=znsl&paperid=2569&option_lang=eng"}> math-net.ru
                ). An English translation appears in the Journal of Soviet Mathematics 6 (1976) pp.422-8. (
                %a{:href => "http://doi.org/10.1007/BF01084083"}> doi
                )
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Peter W. O’Hearn and David J. Pym. The Logic of Bunched Implications.
                %em Bulletin of Symbolic Logic
                5(2):215-244. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.lsv.ens-cachan.fr/~demri/OHearnPym99.pdf"}> pdf
                ) (
                %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.2307%2F421090"}> doi
                )
            %li
              %p
                Stephen Read,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relevant Logic: A Philosophical Examination of Inference
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/~slr/Relevant_Logic.pdf"} pdf
        .revisedby
          %p
